A39 — size the upload buffer from the session's output resolution

The upload buffer was a constant 1920x1080x4, but everything staged through it
is at most one output-sized image: since A33 the session blank is created at
the full output size, so on any output wider than 1080p (2400x1080 phones,
1440p handhelds) the staging request exceeded the buffer and tripped
`assert(size <= upload_buffer_size)` in uploadBufferData, aborting gamescope
before its first frame.

The size is a property of the output, which the host already knows and passes
as -W/-H, so seed the buffer from it: the DRM backend calls vulkan_init (and
therefore CVulkanDevice::BInit) before it picks the mode, so the initial size
comes from g_nPreferredOutputWidth/Height, falling back to the output size and
then to 1080p. If the mode turns out larger anyway, uploadBufferData grows the
buffer rather than asserting — doubling, waiting for the device first, and
retiring the old buffer instead of destroying it, since a command buffer can
still be recording a copy out of it (uploadConstants stages from inside a
recording buffer) and the device has no destruction queue.

The old overflow branch was worse than the assert: it waited and then kept the
offset past the end of the buffer, writing out of bounds.

A37 (android/patches/disabled/) avoided the abort by clearing flat textures
instead of staging them; with the buffer sized correctly that workaround is no
longer needed and is disabled.

